<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
  <%# This page had no heading at all, which #128's rule could not fix on its own -- there
      was nothing for it to apply to, and a page with no title is a skipped level for
      anybody moving through by heading. It gets one here, and the one it gets is a display
      surface: /about is read rather than used, like the marketing page, so it takes the
      serif under #200's rule.

      It obeys the page-title rule at the same time -- an h1 at text-3xl, semibold, in the
      title colour -- which is the point of `serif` being a class rather than a size: a
      thing can be a page title and a display surface at once, and the class decides only
      which face wins. %>
  <h1 class="serif text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900">About</h1>
  <p>Hi! I made an app. If you have questions, find me on twitter <a href="https://twitter.com/stephanieblack" class="font-medium text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">@stephanieblack</a>. I'm happy to help.</p>
  <div>
    <p class="font-medium text-gray-900">Acknowledgements</p>
    <%# Noun Project's licence turns on the credit naming the work in use, and this line
        named a benchpress no page drew while the figure every lift did draw went credited
        nowhere. It now lists the icons actually shipped. Sebastian Schuldt is the name the
        files give: personal-trainer.svg carries "Created by Sebastian Schuldt" and "from
        the Noun Project" in its own text nodes, and all six came off one Illustrator 27.6.1
        export onto the 4:5 canvas Noun Project leaves for the credit, which is the canvas
        that file is on too. That last step is an inference rather than something the repo
        proves -- if one of these turns out to be somebody else's, this is the line that is
        wrong and wants correcting, which is why they are named one at a time rather than
        lumped together as "exercise icons". %>
    <ul class="mt-1 list-disc pl-5">
      <li>Squat, deadlift, benchpress, row, pull-up and barbell icons by Sebastian Schuldt from <a href="https://thenounproject.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Noun Project icons" class="font-medium text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">Noun Project</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>
